`text-emphasis` removes initial `none` and `currentcolor` components even when that changes a rejected shorthand into an accepted one. It also removes `none` before `var()`, which can change the computed value after substitution. This branch checks the shorthand's style and color roles before removing defaults, including the two-token style forms that a color can split.

### Fix

**Turbohtml: keep invalid components and continue reducing valid defaults.** These are complete `minify_css()` outputs for each value in `a{text-emphasis:<value>}` on current main and this branch.

```diff
diff --git a/turbohtml-before.txt b/turbohtml-fixed.txt
--- a/turbohtml-before.txt
+++ b/turbohtml-fixed.txt
@@ -1,10 +1,10 @@
-none sesame: a{text-emphasis:sesame}
-none dot: a{text-emphasis:dot}
-none none: a{text-emphasis:none}
-currentcolor red: a{text-emphasis:red}
-none,red: a{text-emphasis:,red}
-none var(--em): a{text-emphasis:var(--em)}
-open currentcolor dot: a{text-emphasis:open dot}
+none sesame: a{text-emphasis:none sesame}
+none dot: a{text-emphasis:none dot}
+none none: a{text-emphasis:none none}
+currentcolor red: a{text-emphasis:currentcolor red}
+none,red: a{text-emphasis:none,red}
+none var(--em): a{text-emphasis:none var(--em)}
+open currentcolor dot: a{text-emphasis:open currentcolor dot}
 none red: a{text-emphasis:red}
 currentcolor sesame: a{text-emphasis:sesame}
 none #fff: a{text-emphasis:#fff}
```

The [CSS Text Decoration shorthand](https://www.w3.org/TR/css-text-decor-4/#propdef-text-emphasis) permits one emphasis style and one color. Its [style grammar](https://www.w3.org/TR/css-text-decor-4/#propdef-text-emphasis-style) makes `none` exclusive from shapes and strings, and `open dot` is one style component: inserting `currentcolor` between them is invalid. Chrome's `CSS.supports()` rejects the five static values whose old outputs become valid. It rejects `none,red` and `,red`; this branch retains the original invalid declaration. [Variable substitution in shorthands](https://www.w3.org/TR/css-variables-1/#variables-in-shorthands) happens later: with `--em: sesame red`, Chrome computes `none var(--em)` to style `none` and color `rgb(0, 0, 0)`, while the old output computes to style `sesame` and color `rgb(255, 0, 0)`.

### Competitors

**[Lightning CSS 1.33.0](https://github.com/parcel-bundler/lightningcss/releases/tag/v1.33.0) and [cssnano 9.5.0](https://github.com/cssnano/cssnano/releases/tag/cssnano@9.5.0): same as turbohtml** for the six invalid static forms and the `none var(--em)` substitution case above. Lightning CSS also matches `currentcolor sesame`.

**Lightning CSS, different: discard an explicit color next to `none`.** It produces `none` for `none red` and `none #fff`, changing the emphasis color to `currentcolor`. We retain those colors while dropping the redundant style default.

```diff
diff --git a/turbohtml-fixed.txt b/lightningcss.txt
--- a/turbohtml-fixed.txt
+++ b/lightningcss.txt
@@ -1,2 +1,2 @@
-none red: a{text-emphasis:red}
-none #fff: a{text-emphasis:#fff}
+none red: a{text-emphasis:none}
+none #fff: a{text-emphasis:none}
```

**cssnano, different: retain defaults in valid shorthands.** Its outputs retain the parsed value but use more bytes. We keep turbohtml's existing shorter output for these valid cases.

```diff
diff --git a/turbohtml-fixed.txt b/cssnano.txt
--- a/turbohtml-fixed.txt
+++ b/cssnano.txt
@@ -1,3 +1,3 @@
-none red: a{text-emphasis:red}
-currentcolor sesame: a{text-emphasis:sesame}
-none #fff: a{text-emphasis:#fff}
+none red: a{text-emphasis:none red}
+currentcolor sesame: a{text-emphasis:currentcolor sesame}
+none #fff: a{text-emphasis:none #fff}
```

### Validation

Public `minify_css()` cases cover duplicate roles, split styles, separators, CSS-wide keywords, substitutions, functions, and valid reductions. At rebased HEAD `2516c0bf`, full tox passes on Python 3.15 (90,925 tests, 100% Python coverage) and macOS 3.13 (91,383 tests, 100% Python coverage, 45,275/45,275 C branches and 100% C lines). Linux 3.11 runs 91,272 passing tests with 100% Python coverage and 47,692/47,692 C branches; its GCC 16 line gate exits 2 on exactly three uncovered, unchanged lines: `dom/element.c:3557`, `query/xpath/functions.c:654`, and `serialize/unescape.c:299`. Full-file `prek` passes. A Chrome validity sweep checked 1,177 changed two-, three-, and four-token combinations; no before/after validity mismatch remains.

### Performance

Paired Python 3.15 release builds under Callgrind count turbohtml extension instructions against branch base `ac4d4601`. The CodSpeed `minify-css` [Pico CSS 2.0.6 corpus](https://github.com/picocss/pico/blob/v2.0.6/css/pico.css) decreases from 93,691,300 to 93,676,680 over ten complete minifications (−0.016%), with byte-identical output. The 10-rule `minify-css-merges` case decreases from 4,710,700 to 4,706,700 over 100 runs. Over 10,000 isolated calls, `a{color:red}` decreases from 38,560,000 to 38,520,000; valid `a{text-emphasis:none red}` from 51,400,000 to 51,190,000; valid `a{text-emphasis:currentcolor sesame}` from 53,230,000 to 52,250,000; and valid `a{text-decoration:none solid}` from 53,930,000 to 53,370,000.

CodSpeed flags `serialize-inner` (−5.62%) and `serialize-inner-indent` (−5.32%) while warning that the compared jobs used different runtime environments. On the same pinned `html5lib-python` `html.html` corpus (submodule commit `fd4f032`), paired Callgrind runs of ten inner serializations count exactly 28,377,170 turbohtml extension instructions on both base and head. Ten indented inner serializations count exactly 31,874,940 extension instructions on both. Whole-process counts are 42,446,462 → 42,446,179 and 46,563,279 → 46,564,516, respectively. The instruction counts do not reproduce the reported serialization regression.

